SECTION 6: ANTI-HACKER PROVISIONS (MAXIMUM PROTECTION)
===============================================================================

6.1 ABSOLUTE PROHIBITION
All hacking activities are STRICTLY PROHIBITED, including:
- Penetration testing
- Vulnerability scanning
- Exploit development
- Privilege escalation
- Lateral movement
- Data exfiltration
- Persistence mechanisms
- Covering tracks

6.2 NO SAFE HARBOR
There is NO "safe harbor" for:
- Security researchers
- White hat hackers
- Bug bounty hunters
- Academic researchers
- Journalists
- Activists

6.3 RESPONSIBLE DISCLOSURE REQUIRED
If you discover a vulnerability, you MUST:
- Immediately cease all testing
- NOT exploit the vulnerability
- NOT disclose to any third party
- Report responsibly to Owner
- Cooperate with remediation

6.4 CRIMINAL LIABILITY
Hacking activities may constitute criminal offenses under:
- Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA) - USA
- Computer Misuse Act - UK
- Directive 2013/40/EU - EU
- Cybercrime laws in all jurisdictions
- International cybercrime treaties

6.5 CIVIL LIABILITY
In addition to criminal liability, hackers face:
- Compensatory damages
- Statutory damages
- Punitive damages
- Legal fees
- Injunctive relief

6.6 NO STATUTE OF LIMITATIONS
For willful violations, the Owner reserves the right to pursue action 
regardless of time elapsed, to the maximum extent permitted by law.

